summary:
if anthropic is low on compute and still managed to release mythos for enterprise,
then openai should be able to train a model of similar size or even larger with the compute they have.
so i expect openai to train something around mythos level and possibly release it publicly

from my experience so far, this is the frontier ranking:
fable 5 (before ban) &gt;= gpt-5.6 sol &gt; fable 5 (after ban)
but when you factor in token efficiency, gpt-5.6 sol is the better overall pick

clean_summary:
What DeepSeek managed with v4-flash is completely insane. It has roughly 300 billion parameters, using the same architecture and model size as the v4-flash-preview, yet Terminal-Bench scores jumped from 61.8 to 82.7 and DeepSwe from 7.3 to 54.4. All of this at just $0.18 per thousand output tokens.

It's still unclear how post-training alone elevated its performance to Opus 4.8-level while being nearly 100 times cheaper and much smaller.

@deepseek_ai: "DeepSeek-V4-Flash Official API is now live in public beta! We’ve massively upgraded its Agent capabilities—benchmark scores are now far surpassing the V4-Pro-Preview. Check out the massive performance leap below! The official V4-Flash now natively supports the Responses API format and is fully adapted for Codex!"

Grok 4.6 is launching tomorrow if everything goes to plan. It has 1.5 trillion parameters and may challenge Kimi K3 and Opus 4.8 while maintaining Grok 4.5's speed and token efficiency.

Somehow, SpaceXAI has achieved the same level of inference efficiency that OpenAI reached with GPT-5.6.

@elonmusk: "Interesting. Grok 4.6 releases around August 7. This will be the 1.5T model with significantly improved supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning. Grok 4.7, a 2.1T model, will be released a few weeks later, surpassing 4.6 in every way except slightly slower to serve, with even better token efficiency."

Another mathematical milestone. GPT-5.6 Sol and Fable 5 appear to solve a 25-year-old problem in wireless communication theory, with the initial proof produced in around 30 minutes.

It's worth reading the article even without a math background.

@DimitrisPapail: "Last week, GPT-5.6 and Claude Fable appear to have settled an open theoretical question in wireless communications that was intensely studied between 2000 and the 2010s, and that I briefly worked on."
